tickets.defect:
`addons/DynamicAtmManager.cs:497` -- `MonitorTick`, the body of the 5-second `Timer` started by `EnsureMonitor`:

```csharp
#if TESTING
    MonitorTickCore();
#else
    var dispatcher = System.Windows.Application.Current?.Dispatcher;
    if (dispatcher == null) return;
    dispatcher.InvokeAsync(() => MonitorTickCore());
#endif
```

This is `P1-13`'s fail-open verbatim, at a subsystem `P1-13` never inspected. `P1-13` was closed against the guard's own event handlers and its source gate then read only `RiskGuardAddOn.cs`, so this site was inspected by nothing until the gate was widened to the whole addon tree by `P2-29`.

With `Application.Current` null -- early startup before the WPF application object exists, or a headless NT8 -- the monitor returns immediately, FOREVER. Breakeven stops never move to breakeven. Trailing stops never advance. `ReconcileStopFromBroker` never runs, so a refused stop move is never even detected. And nothing anywhere logs a word: this is a protection feature that is silently absent while every surface reports armed and healthy.

SECOND, AND IT IS WHY THIS SURVIVED: the defect lives where no test build can see it. The whole dispatch decision sits behind `#if TESTING`, so the ten existing ATM tests drive `MonitorTickCore()` -- a body the production assembly does not contain. The branch holding the defect exists in NO test build at all. That is `P2-27`'s shape and it must be fixed as part of this, or the tests below cannot express anything.

tickets.spec:
Rewrite the region so that the dispatcher LOOKUP is the only thing behind `#if`, and the control flow is compiled into both builds.

1. Declare an instance field, in this region, immediately above `MonitorTick`:

```csharp
#if TESTING
        internal Func<Action, bool> TryMarshal = _ => false;
#else
        internal Func<Action, bool> TryMarshal = work =>
        {
            var dispatcher = System.Windows.Application.Current?.Dispatcher;
            if (dispatcher == null) return false;
            dispatcher.InvokeAsync(() => work());
            return true;
        };
#endif
```

The contract is: TryMarshal returns TRUE if it has taken ownership of the work and will run it on the UI thread, and FALSE if there is no dispatcher and the caller must run the work itself. The name, the type `Func<Action, bool>` and `internal` visibility are all pinned by the tests -- do not rename, do not make it a property, do not make it static.

2. Declare a `private int` one-shot flag beside it for the announcement.

3. Rewrite `MonitorTick` with NO `#if` in it at all:

   * Call `TryMarshal(MonitorTickCore)`. If it returns true, return -- the dispatcher has it.
   * Otherwise run `MonitorTickCore()` on this thread. This is the fix: with no dispatcher there is nothing to marshal TO, so the only choice is between doing the sweep here and not doing it, and not doing it is the defect.
   * Before running it, announce the fallback EXACTLY ONCE for the life of the process, via `Interlocked.Exchange` on the flag and `RiskGuardAddOn.LogFromComponent(account, eventType, message)` with eventType exactly `ATM_MONITOR_NO_DISPATCHER`. Pass an empty string for the account -- this condition is process-wide, not per-account.
   * Keep the existing try/catch and its `[AtmMonitor] Dispatcher error:` output unchanged.

   The message must say what is now true: the sweep is running on the timer thread rather than being marshalled, breakeven and trailing moves WILL still happen, NT8 broker objects are not thread-safe so an oddly-behaving stop move may be related, and this is reported once per session.

WHY ONCE AND NOT EVERY TICK: `P2-108` on this same codebase was a `NAKED_POSITION` line every 10 seconds forever, and the lesson recorded from it is that an alarm that is always on is off. A 5-second line for the life of the process would replace a silent defect with an unreadable one.

DO NOT: touch any *Tests.cs file; touch MonitorTickCore, EnsureMonitor, RequestStopMove, ModifyStopPrice or ReconcileStopFromBroker; delete the dispatcher path and always run inline (see context -- there is a test that fails if you do); add a NEW `#if TESTING` hook that calls MonitorTick; add a config switch; reformat anything you are not changing.
